# Original Codex rollout feasibility probe

This probe is read-only and does not start Codex, an app server, or a provider-session RPC. It first reads bodyless BB thread metadata and then requests only the server-filtered `thread/identity` and `turn/completed` lanes. The thread must be live, non-archived, and `providerId: codex`; conflicting identities and unrecognized fields fail closed.

Ownership is resolved from the existing router database using `bun:sqlite` with `readonly:true, create:false`. One route must join exactly one enabled, non-draining owner and have no migration claim. The router process and status command are never opened. Owner and filesystem identifiers are transient and are returned only as a SHA-256 account slot.

The already-recorded rollout is admitted only as a regular non-symlink file contained beneath that owner. The reader opens it no-follow, streams it once, verifies UTF-8 and JSONL before interpreting records, caps cumulative bytes at 8 MiB, one line at 4 MiB, records at 512, turn records at 256, and context records at 128. It recognizes only the observed Codex 0.153.2 dialect: `session_meta`, `event_msg`, `response_item`, `world_state`, `turn_context`, and `token_usage_record`. The session metadata must declare 0.153.2. Unknown, malformed, truncated, escaping, nonregular, oversized, or racing input fails closed.

The compact receipt contains only rollout size, digest, record counts, dialect, redacted account slot, and an exact receipt outcome. A Context Magnet receipt is recognized only from a versioned `context_magnet_receipt` item with `context-magnet-receipt/v1`; no timestamp, text, generic developer item, or prompt spelling is inferred. A complete stream with no such item reports `zero-exact-context-receipts`, which proves access feasibility but makes no contribution claim.

The probe compares BB metadata/event high-watermark, owner route revision, direct process inventory, workspace dirty-state identity, and rollout file identity before and after streaming. It never emits provider text, messages, reasoning, commands, tools, environment values, account paths, rollout paths, or raw records.
